[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Serving Static Files (CSS, JavaScript, Images) in Node.js"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Node.js, serving static files like CSS, JavaScript, and images is a common requirement for web applications. This tutorial will guide you through the process of setting up a Node.js server to serve these files using the 'express' framework."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Node.js installed on your system."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"npm (Node Package Manager) installed (comes with Node.js)."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting up the Project"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, create a new directory for your project and navigate into it using the terminal:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"mkdir static-files-example
cd static-files-example"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, initialize a new Node.js project using npm:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"npm init -y"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command creates a 'package.json' file with default settings."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Installing Express"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"We'll use the 'express' framework to simplify the process of creating a web server. Install it using npm:"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"npm install express"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating the Server"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a file named 'server.js' in your project directory. This file will contain the code for our server."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// server.js
const express = require('express');
const path = require('path');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;

// Serve static files from the 'public' directory
app.use(express.static(path.join(__dirname, 'public')));

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server listening at http://localhost:${port}`);
});"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code does the following:"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Imports the 'express' and 'path' modules."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Creates an Express application instance."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Defines the port number the server will listen on (3000 in this case)."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Uses 'express.static()' middleware to serve static files from the 'public' directory.  'path.join(__dirname, 'public')' constructs the absolute path to the 'public' directory."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Starts the server and logs a message to the console."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating the 'public' Directory and Static Files"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a directory named 'public' in your project directory. This directory will hold your static files."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"mkdir public"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inside the 'public' directory, create the following files:"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'style.css' (CSS file)"},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'script.js' (JavaScript file)"},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'image.jpg' (Image file - you can use any image)"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Example contents for 'style.css':"},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"/* style.css */
body {
  font-family: sans-serif;
  background-color: #f0f0f0;
}"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Example contents for 'script.js':"},{"order":34,"type":"code","text":"// script.js
console.log('Static files served successfully!');"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"You'll also need an 'index.html' file in the 'public' directory to display these static files:"},{"order":36,"type":"code","text":"<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Static Files Example</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Serving Static Files in Node.js</h1>
  <img src="image.jpg" alt="Example Image">
  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>"},{"order":37,"type":"subheading","text":"Running the Server"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"In your terminal, run the server using the following command:"},{"order":39,"type":"code","text":"node server.js"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open your web browser and navigate to 'http://localhost:3000'. You should see the 'index.html' page, with the CSS applied, the image displayed, and the JavaScript code executed (check the browser's console)."},{"order":41,"type":"subheading","text":"Explanation"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'express.static()' middleware is crucial here. It tells Express to serve files from the specified directory ('public' in this case) as if they were located at the root of your website. When you request 'http://localhost:3000/style.css', Express looks for a file named 'style.css' in the 'public' directory and serves it to the browser."},{"order":43,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":44,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial demonstrated how to serve static files (CSS, JavaScript, images) in Node.js using the 'express' framework. This is a fundamental concept for building web applications with Node.js."}]
